== A Servant’s Duty and Its Limits under God

=== §. 35. #emph[Of the means to make servants faithful.]

Among many other means to make servants faithful to their master, and careful to perform their other duties sincerely, willingly, cheerfully and diligently, as has been noted before, this is one of the most general: that #emph[servants,] in all things they do for their master, #emph[make their master’s case their own,] and so do for him as they would for themselves, or as they would have their own servants do for them. The general rule of the Law is, “love your neighbor as yourself”;#footnote[Lev 19:18.] and “In everything, do to others as you#super[+] would have them do to you#super[+].”#footnote[Matt 7:12.] If every man must regard another in this way, even though he is a stranger, then much more must servants regard their master, because all that they can do is, in a special way, a debt owed to their masters. In this respect Christ says that #emph[when they have done all that is] #footnote[Luke 17:10.] #emph[commanded, they have only done their duty.]

I have noted this all the more because the practice of most servants is contrary to it. For while they work for their masters, they will cry out that they are overburdened and tired with work which they will easily get through when they work for themselves. Those who must be called to it again and again while they work for their master, and forced to keep at it and hold out till it is done: what pains they will take, how early they will rise, how late they will sit up, how diligent they will be for themselves! When journeymen are paid more according to the work they do, they will get through more than an apprentice who has only clothing, food and lodging. Again, many who are very lavish and wasteful with their master’s goods are very sparing, saving and provident when they come to be their own masters. What do these things show but that servants do not bear toward their masters the mind they should? They do not do for them as they would do for themselves. It is very needful, therefore, that the general rule named above be observed.

So much for the separate kinds of servants’ duties, and the manner of performing them. It remains to speak both of the extent of their duties and of the restraint of that extent.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of servants’ striving to make their judgment agree with their master’s.]

The extent of servants’ duties is only implied in my text, under this indefinite word #emph[(Obey)] (which, not being restricted to any particulars, must be extended to #emph[everything]); but in other places it is expressly laid down in these general terms: “Obey … in all things,”#footnote[Col 3:22.] “please them well in all things.”#footnote[Titus 2:9.] It is not enough, therefore, for servants to perform their duties well in some things; they must do so in all things, even in things that may be against their own mind and liking, if their master will have it so. To this end let servants note these two rules. The same extent and restraint #footnote[Extent of servants’ duties.] that was #footnote[Treatise 3, §. 63, #emph[etc.] & Treatise 5, §. 35.] noted before in setting out the duties of other inferiors is to be observed here also in the duties of servants, for in this they all agree. The same rules, therefore, that were set down before may be applied here again. I refer the reader to those places for a fuller treatment of the general points; here I will content myself with adding such particular proofs as are most proper and pertinent to servants.

1\. That they #emph[labor to bring their judgment into line with their master’s judgment,] and to think that fitting and good which #footnote[Servants must labor to bring their judgment to agree with their master’s.] he does. So the #footnote[Judg 19:11, 12, #emph[etc.]] servant of the Levite, who thought it best to turn into one of the cities of the Jebusites to lodge there because the day was far spent, yielded to his master when he saw him to be of another mind. So did #footnote[Luke 19:25, #emph[etc.]] those servants yield to their master who at first thought it unfitting that he who had ten talents should have one more; and #footnote[Matt 13:28, #emph[etc.]] those servants also who at first thought it fitting that the tares should be pulled up from among the wheat. This rule is to be observed in the particular points dealt with before, such as the work servants do and the manner of doing it, their clothing, their allowance, indeed even the correction their master gives them, and the rest.

Contrary to this is the overweening opinion which many servants have of #footnote[Servants’ overweening opinion of their own understanding.] their own judgment, understanding and wisdom, thinking themselves wiser than their masters. So did Gehazi, who, setting his own understanding against his master’s wisdom, said, “Look, my master has spared this Aramean, Naaman, while not accepting what he brought. As surely as the #smallcaps[Lord] lives, I will run after him and get something from him.”#footnote[2 Kgs 5:20.] This was also a fault in #footnote[2 Kgs 2:16.] the sons of the prophets, who would not rest on Elisha’s judgment, but pressed him against his mind to send some men to look for the body of Elijah. This self-conceit is the cause of many harms: of discontent at the work their master appoints them and at the allowance of food and drink they have; of the great liberty they take to themselves; of pride in clothing; and of other vices noted before. If the rule named above were duly observed, many of those harms would be avoided, and much better obedience given.

=== §. 37. #emph[Of servants’ yielding to do such things at their master’s command as they cannot think to be most fitting.]

The second rule which servants must observe is this:

#emph[Though in their judgment they cannot think that what their master wants done is fit to be done, yet at his peremptory command they must yield to doing it.] It appears from Peter’s answer to Christ that he did so. For when he was commanded to let down his net for a catch, he answered like this: “Master, we have worked hard all night without catching anything”#footnote[Luke 5:5.] (here he shows that his opinion was that it would be in vain to let down their nets); “But because You say so, I will let down the nets” (here is his obedience against his opinion). The example of Joab is clearer for this purpose. When the king commanded him to number the #footnote[2 Sam 24:2, #emph[etc.]] people, he made it plain that he thought it a very unfitting thing to do, by this phrase: “But why does my lord the king want to do such a thing?” Yet against his judgment he yielded to the king’s peremptory command; for it is said that “the king’s word prevailed against Joab.”

#emph[Objection.] This is no fit example, because Joab sinned in obeying.

#emph[Answer.] Joab cannot justly be charged with sin, because it was not in itself unlawful to number the people. David’s sin was not in the act of numbering the people, but in the mind that moved him to do it. There was no just cause to do it; only pride and curiosity moved him, as may be gathered from his own reason in these words: “so that I may know their number.” To do such a thing only to know it was #emph[curiosity.] But why would he know it? Surely from a proud thought that he had so many mighty men, so many captains, so many men of war.

From Joab’s example servants may learn here to give, in humility and reverence, some reasons to move their master not to press on them what they think unfitting. But if their master will not listen to their reasons, but stands on his authority, #emph[his word must prevail.]

Contrary to this is the peremptoriness of those who can by no means be drawn to do anything at their master’s command which they themselves do not think most fitting to be done. Such a one is that fool whom Solomon describes in this way: “Though you #footnote[Prov 27:22.] grind a fool in a mortar with a pestle along with grain, yet his folly will not depart from him.” These fools bring much injury on their own heads by disobeying their masters, for they greatly provoke the wrath of him who has power to take vengeance on them. Nor let them expect to receive comfort in their suffering because they refuse an unfitting thing; for #emph[fitness] is not a sufficient warrant against #emph[unlawfulness.] To disobey in a thing which may lawfully be done is unlawful. If, therefore, the pretext is only #emph[an unfitting thing,] fitness is preferred to lawfulness, and unlawfulness is counted a lesser matter than unfitness.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of servants’ refraining from obeying their master against God.]

So that the extent of servants’ obedience is not stretched too far, #footnote[Restraint of the extent of servants’ duties] the apostle sets down an excellent limitation of it, and that in these four phrases: #emph[as to Christ, as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God, as to the Lord.] All of these show that the #emph[obedience which servants give to their master] #footnote[See the cautions, Treatise 3, §. 51, 52, etc.] #emph[must be such as may stand with their obedience to Christ.] So if masters command their servants ever so peremptorily to do any unlawful thing, that is, anything forbidden by God’s #footnote[#emph[Nolite fieri serui hominum, i. nolite eis parere cùm turpia praecipiunt. Chrys. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[hom.] 19. \[Do not become servants of men; that is, do not obey them when they command shameful things.\] Exod 1:17.] word, they must not yield to it. The midwives of the Hebrew women did well in refusing to do anything to further the murderous practices of the king of Egypt in killing all the male children of the Hebrews. It is expressly said that they “feared God and did not do as the king of Egypt had instructed”; so their disobedience in this matter was a token of their fear of God. In this case Joseph is commended for not listening to his mistress, and the servants of Saul for refusing to kill the #footnote[Gen 39:9. 1 Sam 22:17.] Lord’s priests at their master’s command. So if a master should command his servant to kill, to steal, to swear falsely, to lie, to use false measures and weights, to go to Mass, or to do any other unlawful thing, he ought not to obey him.

Again, if masters forbid their servants to do what #footnote[Seruis competit vt obediant dominis in ijs quibus mandato Dei nihil derogatur. Basil. #emph[Ethic. defin.] 75. \[It is fitting for servants to obey their masters in those things in which nothing is taken away from God’s command.\]] God has commanded them to do, they must do it in spite of their masters’ prohibition. The rulers of Israel forbade the apostles to preach; yet because Christ had commanded them to preach, they would not refrain.#footnote[Acts 4:19.] Nor would Daniel refrain from praying to God, though the king and nobles forbade him by express decree.#footnote[Dan 6:10.] So if a profane or popish master forbids his servant to go to church, or to hear the word, or to take the sacrament, or to live with his wife if he is married, or to make restitution of what he has gotten by fraud, or any other duty to which he is bound, in this servants must say, “We must obey God rather than men.”#footnote[Acts 5:29.] For when masters command and forbid anything against God, they go beyond their commission, and in that their authority ceases.

Contrary to this restraint are both a sycophantic pleasing of masters and a base fear of them. It is the mark of a #footnote[Ais? aio. Negas? nego. Terent. in Eun.] sycophant to say what his master would have him say, and deny what he would have him deny, and so to do what he would have him do. Doeg, that fawning dog, killed all the Lord’s #footnote[1 Sam 22:18.] priests at Saul’s word; and Absalom’s servants killed Amnon at his word. For #footnote[2 Sam 13:28, 29.] all the reason they had to commit that murder was this speech of their master: “Have I not commanded you#super[+]?” Servants are so prone to flatter their masters that there is no sin so horrible that they will not be ready to do it at their masters’ command. In this way what the prophet foretold long ago is proved true: #footnote[Isa 24:2.] “like master, like servant; like mistress, like maid.” It is also the mark of base, fearful servants to do nothing but what their master would have them do, and to leave undone any duty, however necessary, which he forbids them to do. What duty is more necessary than prayer? We are commanded to “pray without ceasing”;#footnote[1 Thess 5:17. Dan 6:7, #emph[etc.]] yet the servants of Darius were content to leave that duty undone for thirty days on end, because it was against the decree of their lord and king. The like is noted of the people in Christ’s time: #emph[they dared not openly confess] #footnote[John 7:13; 9:22.] #emph[Christ for fear of the Jews.] So in our days there are many servants who dare not profess the gospel, or go to church, or read the word, or perform other holy religious duties which they know to be binding duties, for fear of their masters. Did the apostle not have just cause, then, to strike so much on this string as he has done here, expressly forbidding #emph[eye-service, man-pleasing] and #emph[doing service to men,] namely in opposition to God?

=== §. 39. #emph[Of servants’ choosing good masters.]

As a just consequence following from the extent and restraint of servants’ duties named above, I may further gather these two lessons for servants.

1\. It is very needful that servants choose good masters, at least if it is in their power to choose.

2\. It is needful that they continue and stay under good masters, at least if they continue as servants and stay under any masters.

That these consequences justly follow, as said above, is evident. For seeing that servants are bound to obey in all things which are not #emph[against God,] and must obey in nothing but what is #emph[in the Lord,] it is very needful that servants be under such masters as bear the image of God in the inward disposition and grace of their heart, as well as in their outward function and place; masters who will go along with God in using their authority, commanding nothing but what a servant may do with a good conscience and without transgressing against God, and forbidding nothing that God has bound a servant to do. There will be comfort in serving such masters, and our obedience to them will be obedience to God. Such masters, therefore, must be chosen. Indeed, with such masters servants must stay (if not with the very same, yet with such as they are, of the same disposition). For if they go to others, their former comfort will be taken away, and their servitude will seem all the more miserable, the more knowledge and experience they have had of the benefit of their former liberty. (For we may well call service under masters without conscience #emph[servitude,] and, in contrast to it, service under religious masters #emph[liberty.]) He therefore who has a master that is faithful must, with due respect to his authority, love him as a father,#footnote[Qui fidelem habet dominum, saluo eius dominio, diligat vt patrem. #emph[Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12.] and so stay with him.

For the choice of good masters, note what is #footnote[Exod 12:38.] recorded of many of the Egyptians and other peoples: they left their own country and went out with the Israelites. What could move them but awareness of the true God, whom they knew Israel served? Now many of these went out as servants, as may be gathered from those many #footnote[Lev 25:45. Deut 5:14; 12:18; 23:15; 23:7.] laws which were made on behalf of servants who were strangers, and especially those who were Egyptians. The knowledge Ruth#footnote[Ruth 1:16.] had that Naomi, her mother-in-law, served the true God moved her to leave her own country and to go as a servant with Naomi.

For staying with good masters we have a worthy pattern in the twelve disciples. #footnote[John 6:66. #emph[etc.]] When many of Christ’s disciples in the wider sense “turned back and no longer walked with Him,” and Christ asked the twelve whether they too would go away, Peter, in the name of all the rest, answered, “Lord, to whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life.”

Contrary to this is the mind and practice of many servants: they never inquire into the religious disposition of their master, nor care if he is popish or profane, so long as they may have good wages, meals and lodging. Yet by this means, if there is at least any spark of the fear of God in their heart, they expose themselves to many severe temptations, and bring themselves into many hard straits and dangerous snares. And if God opens their eyes to see the wretched condition into which they have plunged themselves, they will be forced to cry out and complain as David did when he was in foreign countries where he could not freely serve his God: “Woe to me that I dwell in Meshech, that I live among the tents of Kedar!”#footnote[Ps 120:5.]

Much more contrary is the mind and disposition of those who refuse to serve religious masters and shun them most of all, or who, being in their service, are never at rest till they are out of it again. We spoke of these #footnote[§. 16.] before.

So far we have dealt with servants’ duties. The reasons which the apostle gives to enforce those duties remain to be set out.

=== §. 40. #emph[Of the first motive, the place of masters.]

The first reason the apostle uses to enforce servants’ #footnote[I.] duties is implied in this phrase, #emph[as to Christ.] It points to the #emph[place] of a master, which is to be in #emph[Christ’s stead.] In this respect the title #emph[Lord] is given to a master. The word the apostle uses in Greek is the one which in the New Testament #footnote[Κύριος.] answers to that proper Hebrew name of God, #emph[Jehovah,] and it sets out the absolute sovereignty of God and his power over all creatures.#footnote[Matt 28:18.] Because the Father has given this power to his Son as Mediator, God-man, he is called the #footnote[1 Cor 8:6 εἷς κύριος.] #emph[one] or #emph[only Lord.] And because masters, by virtue of their office and place, bear Christ’s image and stand in his stead, through Christ’s imparting his authority to them, they are called #emph[lords,] indeed even #emph[gods]#footnote[Ps 82:6.] (for what a magistrate is in the commonwealth, a master is in the family).

Hence it follows that servants, in performing duty to their master, perform duty to Christ, and in rebelling against their master they rebel against Christ; as the Lord said to Samuel about the people’s rejecting his government, “it is not #footnote[1 Sam 8:7.] you they have rejected, but they have rejected Me as their king.” Is this not a strong motive to stir servants up to every duty, and to restrain them from rebellion? If one weighs well what Christ is, it cannot but be a weighty reason. For though masters should neither reward their good service nor punish their bad service, yet Christ will do both.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of the second motive, the place of servants.]

The second reason is implied in this phrase, #emph[as the servants] #footnote[II.] #emph[of Christ.] It points to the place of a servant, which, though it may seem a lowly and base place, is in fact an honorable place. Men count it an honor to be servants to a king; but Christ is higher than all kings. On this ground the apostle says, “Were you a servant when you were called? Do not let it concern you,”#footnote[1 Cor 7:21, 22.] and that for this reason: “he who was a servant when he was called by the Lord is the Lord’s freeman.” This reason is to be noted against the notion most people have of a servant’s place, that it is so lowly and base that there can be no honor or comfort in doing its duties. But that is a foolish and unjust notion. Look to Christ, the highest master, and there is as much honor and comfort in doing the duties of the lowest servant’s place as of the highest master’s.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of the third motive, God’s will.]

The third reason is implied in this phrase, #emph[doing the] #footnote[III.] #emph[will of God,] which declares the ground of servants’ subjection. God in his word has plainly made it known that it is his pleasure that those who are under the authority of masters should obey them. Therefore, if servants would please God, they must obey; if they refuse to obey, they thwart the #emph[will of God.] This also is a weighty motive. For God’s will is the mark which everyone ought to aim at, and the Holy Spirit urges it much as a general reason for all duty, in these and similar phrases: “This is the will of God,”#footnote[1 Thess 4:3.] “Such is the will of God.”#footnote[1 Pet 2:15.] On this ground we are exhorted to “understand”#footnote[Eph 5:17.] and to “test and approve what the will of God is.”#footnote[Rom 12:2.]

There is good reason to press this reason:

1\. God’s will is the very ground of goodness: things are good for this reason, that they agree with God’s will. God’s will gives goodness its very being.

2\. God’s will is a rule to square all our actions by, just as the king’s statutes and proclamations are to his subjects.

3\. It is a perfect rule (“the Law of the #smallcaps[Lord] is perfect”),#footnote[Ps 19:7.] so that we may be sure not to swerve if we hold close to it.

4\. It is a sufficient rule: it will give everyone (and servants among others) direction how to conduct themselves in everything they take in hand, indeed in everything that belongs to them. For God’s word is given to make us “complete, fully #footnote[2 Tim 3:17.] equipped for every good work.”

5\. It is a good warrant to justify us in all our actions, so that, going along with it, we need not care what any man can say against us. If a man is sure that he has statute law or the king’s proclamation on his side, he is bold.

From this reason, which is of such weight, I gather two propositions to add force to this motive.

1\. That servants obey their masters is no optional matter but a necessary duty: not left to a servant’s will whether he will do it or not, but a thing to which he is bound; and that not only by the civil laws of men, but also by a divine institution of God. So it is not only a matter of public order but also of conscience, to be done for conscience’ sake.

2\. That no created being can grant servants a dispensation, so that they should be exempted from doing their duty to their master. If any could, they would be greater than God, and their will above God’s will. Masters themselves are to be reckoned among created beings. Now because it is God’s will that servants should be in subjection, their masters cannot exempt them from it. Masters may set them free; but while they keep them as servants, they cannot exempt them from a servant’s subjection. Therefore, though masters are careless in demanding duty, let servants be conscientious in giving duty, because it is God’s will.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of the fourth reason, the reward of good service.]

The fourth reason is plainly and fully expressed in these #footnote[IIII.] words: “Knowing that whatever good thing any man does, the #footnote[#emph[Sciat seruus Deum redditurum seruitutis mercedem. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12. Col 3:24.] same he will receive from the Lord, whether he is slave or free.” Its general sum is a #emph[declaration of the reward of good service.] To this purpose the apostle says to servants in another place, “From the Lord you#super[+] will receive a reward.”

This is to be noted first, to add force to the former reasons. For if anyone asks, what if masters do stand in Christ’s stead, what has that to do with the matter? Surely it has much to do with moving servants, because masters stand in the place of him who will reward what is done for his sake.

If again they ask, what benefit is it to be Christ’s servant? It may be answered: #emph[very great;] for Christ will reward all his servants.

If it is further asked, what is gained by doing God’s will?

#emph[Answer.] Just what all aim at: reward for service.

As this adds weight to all the other reasons, so it is in itself a weighty reason, as weighty as any can be to our corrupt nature. It is hope of reward that makes all sorts of people take the pains they do in their separate places. But having #footnote[Treatise 1, §. 127.] dealt with this general point of reward before, I will show here more distinctly what kind of reward it is with which the Lord will recompense the good service of faithful servants.

God’s reward consists of both temporal and eternal blessings.

The temporal blessings which God has bestowed in the past #footnote[What reward God gives to good servants.] on faithful servants, and which other such servants may on that ground expect, are especially these.

1\. He will move masters to show regard for such servants, as he moved #footnote[Esth 6:3.] Ahasuerus to recompense the faithfulness of Mordecai.

2\. If masters fail in this, he will move strangers to recompense them, as he moved #footnote[Gen 39:21.] the jailer to favor Joseph when his master had cast him into prison, and Pharaoh#footnote[Gen 41:40.] to raise him to great dignity.

3\. To draw the hearts of their masters and others more to them, he will make the things they take in hand prosper. In this way he blessed Joseph#footnote[Gen 39:2.] and Abraham’s#footnote[Gen 24:56.] servant.

4\. When they work for themselves, he will bless their labors, as he blessed Jacob’s.#footnote[Gen 30:43.]

5\. When they come to keep servants, he will provide such servants for them as they were to their masters. #footnote[Gen 43:23.] In Egypt God blessed Joseph with a faithful servant. David, who #footnote[1 Sam 17:34.] risked his life to save his father’s sheep, had #footnote[2 Sam 21:17; 23:15.] many servants who risked their lives for him.

To this purpose may be applied that proverb which Christ often uses: “with the measure you#super[+] use, it will be measured back to you#super[+].”#footnote[Luke 6:38.]

The eternal reward is expressed in this phrase, “the reward of the inheritance.”#footnote[Col 3:24.] For the inheritance meant is the one Christ mentions in this clause, “Come, you#super[+] who are blessed by My Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you#super[+] from the foundation of the world,”#footnote[Matt 25:34.] and Saint Peter in this, “an inheritance that is imperishable, undefiled, and unfading, reserved in heaven.”#footnote[1 Pet 1:4.] What more can be said? What will not be undergone for this reward? How willingly do apprentices go through their apprenticeship in hope of a temporary freedom of an earthly city! #footnote[Gen 29:20.] Many years’ service was only like a few days to Jacob, because he liked his reward. But see here a greater reward, which we must surely like much better. If this is not enough to move servants to every duty, I do not know what can be enough.
